WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.640
"Transformers: Rise of the Beasts" introduces&nbsp;
a new set of robots in disguise, but it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.660
place in the 1990s, well before the&nbsp;
entries in the series directed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.160
Michael Bay. So why didn't the Autobots&nbsp;
ever mention their pals the Maximals?

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.680
Spoilers for "Rise of the Beasts" ahead!

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.300
The continuity of the live-action "Transformers"&nbsp;
films has always been pretty sketchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:27.120
even within the five directed by Michael Bay.&nbsp;
2018's "Bumblebee," which took place in 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.100
ostensibly functions as a soft reboot of&nbsp;
the franchise, but it's also a prequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.720
showing what Bumblebee was up to before he met&nbsp;
Sam Witwicky in the first "Transformers" movie.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.280
"You talk through the radio?"

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.640
"Thank you, you're beautiful.&nbsp;
You're wonderful, you're wonderful."

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.180
"Rise of the Beasts" supposedly takes&nbsp;
place within this same timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.440
with the previous cinematic installment&nbsp;
being directly referenced when Optimus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.500
Prime mentions how Bumblebee had&nbsp;
already made friends with humans.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.940
Connecting the two most recent "Transformers"&nbsp;
movies with Michael Bay's entries in the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.100
can prove troublesome, especially now that the&nbsp;
Maximals have been introduced. They've been on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.360
Earth for a long time, and after the climactic&nbsp;
battle, it's assumed they're still around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:12.000
probably continuing to be based in Peru. So&nbsp;
if "Rise of the Beasts" leads into Bay films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.660
it raises the question of why the&nbsp;
Maximals didn't help out during all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.500
of those potential world-ending events.&nbsp;
The Maximals are even the ones who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.000
to teach Optimus Prime to work alongside the&nbsp;
humans, so they likely have just as much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.020
an interest in protecting humanity, if not&nbsp;
more. Why did they remain in hiding then?

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.400
After the good guys succeed in destroying&nbsp;
the Transwarp Key and preventing Unicron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.780
from coming to Earth, everyone seemingly&nbsp;
goes their separate ways. The Autobots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.440
are more accepting of humanity, while&nbsp;
the human characters are changed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.520
the better. It's unclear what exactly&nbsp;
the Maximals get up to, but presumably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.420
they go back to living in the Peruvian jungle&nbsp;
with the indigenous tribe they befriended.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.560
It's understandable that they don't necessarily&nbsp;
want to be bothered with every little thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.980
but the Michael Bay "Transformers"&nbsp;
movies have some significant threats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.760
that put the entire world in jeopardy.&nbsp;
It's unclear why the Autobots wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.460
call on them again to help them&nbsp;
to fight off the Decepticons.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.900
"Roll out!"
 

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.580
There may be another factor at play here, though:&nbsp;
No one can seem to decide if "Bumblebee" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.660
"Rise of the Beasts" are prequels to the Bay&nbsp;
films straight-up reboots. Some things are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:17.220
seemingly canon. Megatron, for example, wasn't&nbsp;
in "Bumblebee" or "Rise of the Beasts" because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.420
the other entries establish he was frozen&nbsp;
during those decades. "Bumblebee" director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.980
Travis Knight even told The Hollywood Reporter he&nbsp;
couldn't use Megatron in his film for that reason.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.000
On the other hand, there's a lot that's seemingly&nbsp;
being retconned in the new "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.680
movies. For one, 2017's "The Last Knight"&nbsp;
established how Unicron was actually Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.820
with its horns protruding out of the planet's&nbsp;
surface. "Rise of the Beasts," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.620
shows Unicron as a completely separate entity.&nbsp;
The new movie also has a final scene where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.360
Noah is recruited by G.I. Joe, potentially&nbsp;
setting up a crossover event between the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.220
Hasbro franchises. This would only open up more&nbsp;
questions as to how the movies fit in with Bay's.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.540
It's possible the explanation for why the&nbsp;
Maximals aren't in any other "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.860
movie is that these new ones take place&nbsp;
in a different timeline. If Paramount does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.820
want to keep everything under one roof, the&nbsp;
Maximals' exclusion could be explained in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.660
future installment. Perhaps they sacrifice&nbsp;
their lives valiantly to protect Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.740
or maybe they find a way off the planet. At&nbsp;
the end of the day, these are movies about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.240
robot aliens that turn into cars, so maybe&nbsp;
it's best not to think too hard about it.

